{"name":"Masked Mauve","hex":"#c6b2be","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"masked","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["simple past and past participle of mask"]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Wearing a mask or masks.","Characterized by masks."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/masked"},{"term":"mauve","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A rich purple synthetic dye, which faded easily, briefly popular c. 1859‒1873 and now called mauveine.","A pale purple or violet colour, like the colour of the dye after it has faded."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a pale purple colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mauve"}],"entries":[{"title":"Mask","blocks":[{"text":"A mask is an object normally worn on the face, typically for protection, disguise, performance, or entertainment, and often employed for rituals and rites. Masks have been used since antiquity for both ceremonial and practical purposes, as well as in the performing arts and for entertainment. They are usually worn on the face, though they may also be positioned elsewhere on the wearer's body for effect."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Ravensburg_Fasnet_Papierkrattler_2005.jpg/330px-Ravensburg_Fasnet_Papierkrattler_2005.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mask","wikidata":"Q161524"},{"title":"Mauve","blocks":[{"text":"Mauve is a pale purple color named after the mallow flower. The first use of the word mauve as a color was in 1796–1798 according to the Oxford English Dictionary, but its use seems to have been rare before 1859. Another name for the color is mallow, with the first recorded use of mallow as a color name in English in 1611."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Wilde_Malve.JPG/330px-Wilde_Malve.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","lin